This course applies rigorous economics analysis to important features of major organizations in society, especially modern firms. The material is organized into the following modules: 
• Why do you set up a firm? -- Entrepreneurs and boundaries of the firm;
• How to attract and motivate the right people to work for the firm? -- Incentives and employment in organizations;
• How to get things done in firms? -- Decision-making and implementation in organizations;
• Is the firm fulfilling its purpose? -- Evaluating the performance of organizations.

This is an advanced-level course covering traditional topics as well as research topics that have arisen in response to recent world developments in the discipline of trade and growth. Why some nations are rich and others are poor is a central question in the study of economics. This course draws upon the student’s prior training in standard theory and empirics to examine some key questions that have first-order effects on human welfare.

Nine key topics will be covered in this course:
1. Rare Disasters, Asset Markets, and Equity Premium;
2. Ideas and Population;
3. Rural-Urban Migration and Cities in Economic Growth;
4. Industrialization and the Big Push;
5. Natural Resources and the Limits to Growth;
6. Terms of Trade and Economic Growth in the International Economy;
7. Trade and Technological Diffusion;
8. Natural Selection and Economic Growth; and
9. Economic Justice in the International Economy.

This course will introduce the foundational models used in the study of modern economic growth and examine selected topics on growth. It will cover the following: 
1. The Solow growth model and its success in explaining empirical facts; 
2. Endogenous growth: Capital accumulation and externalities; 
3. Endogenous growth with expanding input varieties; 
4. Global interdependencies: Technological diffusion, trade and the world distribution of income; 
5. Productivity differences across countries; 
6. Quantity and quality of life and economic growth

This course looks at the essential aspects of financial decision-making. The objective is to inculcate an understanding of the elements of strategic financial management and to build proficiency in their application to real world problems. Participants will be provided with a rigorous introduction to the foundations of business finance in competitive financial markets. The course is designed to equip managers and leaders with the basic tools that are necessary for managing a firm in a dynamic financial environment. Emphasis will be placed on applying these tools to deriving optimal decision strategies that will maximize the value of the firm. 

This course is a blend of theory and practice where the focus is on: 
1.	Appreciating the financial environment that a firm operates in;
2.	Understanding the key drivers of firm value;
3.	Learning the underlying financial theories that drive practice;
4.	Maximizing the value of a firm’s stakeholders.

The financial market is filled with risks.  Oftentimes, those risks are significantly elevated by a lack of some basic but critical understanding of why and how the market moves, a process we will call market dynamics.  The purpose of this course is to equip participants with the ability to decipher market dynamics. 

At the end of the course, participants will be able to: 
(1)	Determine what moves the market and why;  
(2)	How to stay close to the market and feel its pulse;   
(3)	Make informed decisions about market movements; and
(4)	Make an educated conjecture about evolving trends.

